=========================================================
OPERATOR OVERLOADING - QUICK REVISION ⭐⭐⭐⭐⭐
=========================================================

1. Assignment (=)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Copy one object to another.

Syntax:
Class& operator=(const Class& other);

Example:
obj1 = obj2;

Interview Point:
✓ Invoked on assignment.
✓ Handle self-assignment.
✓ Return *this.

=========================================================

2. Dereference (*)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Access the object pointed to by a pointer-like object.

Syntax:
T& operator*();

Example:
SmartPtr ptr(new Employee);
(*ptr).display();

Interview Point:
✓ Returns reference to underlying object.
✓ Commonly used in smart pointers.

=========================================================

3. Arrow (->)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Access members through pointer-like objects.

Syntax:
T* operator->();

Example:
SmartPtr ptr(new Employee);
ptr->display();

Interview Point:
✓ Must return a pointer (or another object with operator->()).
✓ Used by smart pointers and STL iterators.

=========================================================

4. Subscript ([])
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Access container elements.

Syntax:
T& operator[](int index);

Example:
arr[2] = 100;
cout << arr[2];

Interview Point:
✓ Should return reference for modification.
✓ Provide const overload for read-only objects.

=========================================================

5. Function Call (())
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Make an object behave like a function.

Syntax:
ReturnType operator()(arguments);

Example:
class Compare
{
public:
    bool operator()(int a, int b)
    {
        return a > b;
    }
};

Compare cmp;
cmp(10,20);

Interview Point:
✓ Creates functors.
✓ Can store state.
✓ Used in sort(), priority_queue, std::thread.

=========================================================

6. Stream Insertion (<<)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Print objects using cout.

Syntax:
friend ostream&
operator<<(ostream&, const Class&);

Example:
cout << obj;

Interview Point:
✓ Usually friend.
✓ Return ostream& for chaining.

=========================================================

7. Stream Extraction (>>)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Read objects using cin.

Syntax:
friend istream&
operator>>(istream&, Class&);

Example:
cin >> obj;

Interview Point:
✓ Usually friend.
✓ Return istream& for chaining.

=========================================================

8. Pre-increment (++obj)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Increment then return object.

Syntax:
Class& operator++();

Example:
++obj;

Interview Point:
✓ No dummy parameter.
✓ Return reference.

=========================================================

9. Post-increment (obj++)
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Return old value, then increment.

Syntax:
Class operator++(int);

Example:
obj++;

Interview Point:
✓ int is a dummy parameter.
✓ Return old copy.

=========================================================

10. new / delete
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Customize memory allocation.

Syntax:
void* operator new(size_t size);
void operator delete(void* ptr);

Example:
Employee* e = new Employee;
delete e;

Interview Point:
✓ Used for memory pools.
✓ Custom allocators.

=========================================================

11. Type Conversion
---------------------------------------------------------
Purpose:
Convert object to another type.

Syntax:
operator int();
operator string();

Example:
Employee e;
int id = e;

Interview Point:
✓ Can cause implicit conversions.
✓ Use explicit constructor to prevent unwanted conversions.

